content.text:
## Root cause

Only **Case 2** (both branches) can ever fire with `sibling == t->nil`: `t->nil` self-references its own `left`/`right`, both "black," so nil trivially satisfies Case 2's `sibling->left->color == BLACK && sibling->right->color == BLACK` check — and then unconditionally does `sibling->color = RB_RED`. Every other color-write in the loop targets a node that's provably real at that point (Case 1 requires the sibling already be `RED`, which `t->nil` never legitimately is; Case 3/4's targets are provably non-nil once you trace the preceding conditions). So the fix is two guards, at [rbtree.c:294](src/rbtree.c#L294) and [rbtree.c:323](src/rbtree.c#L323).

## Proposed edit

```c
                if (sibling->left->color == RB_BLACK && sibling->right->color == RB_BLACK) {
                    // Case 2
-                   sibling->color = RB_RED;
+                   if (sibling != t->nil)   /* t->nil is an immutable black constant */
+                       sibling->color = RB_RED;
                    fixNode = fixNode->parent;
```
and the mirror:
```c
                if (sibling->right->color == RB_BLACK && sibling->left->color == RB_BLACK) {
                    // Case 2
-                   sibling->color = RB_RED;
+                   if (sibling != t->nil)   /* t->nil is an immutable black constant */
+                       sibling->color = RB_RED;
                    fixNode = fixNode->parent;
```

